==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|futtek}}, {{m|enm|votek}}, of uncertain origin. Perhaps from a Middle English compound equivalent to {{compound|en|foot|hook}}; or from {{der|en|dum|voetkijn}}, diminutive of {{m|dum|voet|t=foot}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|foot|kin|nocat=1}}; or alternatively, from {{inh|en|ang|*fōtuc}}, equivalent to {{suffix|en|foot|ock|pos2=diminutive suffix}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} Any of the [[curved]] [[timber]]s that form the [[rib]]s of a [[ship]].
:: {{quote-book|year=1884|author={{w|Dixon Kemp}}|title=A Manual of Yacht and Boat Sailing (Fourth Edition)|page=12|passage=The timbers (called also frames, or floors, first, second and third '''futtocks''', where the lengths of the frames are in two, three, or more pieces) will be "double," that is, two timbers will be placed close together, or nearly close together, and act as one frame.}}

